{
 "entry": 142,
 "date": "2026-09-15",
 "box": "211",
 "description": "The Prym test on the route-less families with small components (entry 142).  For each family the component Gamma (a plane curve in the frame ratios g, h) carries the sign-and-inversion group G = {1, joint, diag(1,1), diag(-1,-1)} = (Z/2)^2 (joint: (g,h) -> (-g,-h); diag(s,s): (g,h) -> (s/g, s/h); joint o diag(1,1) = diag(-1,-1)).  Kani-Rosen: J(Gamma) x J(Gamma/G)^2 ~ J(Gamma/H1) x J(Gamma/H2) x J(Gamma/H3) over Q, and J(Gamma/H) ~ J(Gamma/G) x P_H with P_H the Prym part; so every simple isogeny factor of J(Gamma) over Q is a factor of J(Gamma/G) or of one of the three P_H.  For a prime p at which the reduction of a curve keeps its genus (checked), its Jacobian has good reduction and the characteristic polynomial of Frobenius is the L-polynomial computed from the numbers of places of degree <= d of the function field over GF(p) (d = the genus, or the dimension of the Prym part after subtracting the power sums of the full quotient's Frobenius eigenvalues); an isogeny factor of dimension e over Q would give a factor of degree 2e over Q of that polynomial.  Result: at the witness primes every piece has no irreducible factor of degree <= 4, so J(Gamma) has no isogeny factor of dimension 1 or 2 over Q, and Gamma has no non-constant map over Q to any curve of genus 1 or 2.",
 "tools": "SageMath 10.7 (function fields over GF(p): genus, exact constant field, places by degree; compute/qc/prym_test.sage), the Magma online calculator for the hyperellipticity and the first L-polynomials (compute/qc/magma_tower142.out.txt, magma_tower142b.out.txt)",
